{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1068319-mtaw-1st-edition-sensory-range-sensory-magic-and-the-200-yard-rule","id":1068319,"title":"MtAw; 1st edition; Sensory Range, Sensory Magic, and the 200-yard rule","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-03-26T09:10:50-07:00","id":1068319,"title":"MtAw; 1st edition; Sensory Range, Sensory Magic, and the 200-yard rule","content":"<span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#231f20\">&quot;<u><b>Most spells rely on a mage\u2019s mundane senses</b>.</u> He can cast a spell on any target distinguishable by sight, hearing or touch and who\u2019s in the mage\u2019s presence. If there\u2019s some question as to whether or not the mage can distinguish the target, the Storyteller may apply a penalty to the mage\u2019s spellcasting pool.&quot; - MtAw, 1st Ed. Core (114)<br /> <br /> &quot;<u><b>If there\u2019s some question as to whether or not the mage can distinguish the target, the Storyteller may apply a penalty to the mage\u2019s spellcasting pool.</b></u> If a mage cannot see his target, the rules for listening in the </span><i><span style=\"color:#231f20\">World of Darkness Rulebook</span></i><span style=\"color:#231f20\">(pp. 166-167) can be used to target someone through sound, which usually involves penalties for concealment. Likewise, if he\u2019s got advanced olfactory senses, the rules for smelling (</span><i><span style=\"color:#231f20\">World of Darkness Rulebook</span></i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:#231f20\"><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">, p. 167) can be used. Neither of these methods is as ideal as sight, since even the best attempt to pinpoint a target gives him partial concealment (a \u20132 dice penalty if the mage gets an exceptional success on his listening or smelling attempt).&quot; - MtAw 1st Ed. Core (114)<br /> <br /> &quot;<u><b>Targets that cannot be sensed, or that can be sensed but are farther than 200 yards away, or that are visible or audible to the mage but not in his presence</b></u> \u2014 seen on live television or heard over the telephone, for example \u2014 require sympathetic spellcasting. In the case of someone seen or heard live over the television or telephone, the sensory impression of the target provides a strong connection (see below).&quot; - MtAw 1st Ed. Core (114)<br /> <br /> &quot;Sense Consciousness (Mind 1) - <u><b>The mage can detect the presence of minds within sensory range</b>.</u> Successes are used to pierce any supernatural occlusion a mind may possess. While the spell is in effect, it creates a \u201csafety zone\u201d around the mage into which no mind can enter without his knowledge.&quot; &quot;The mage detects the general type of mind she senses (a ghost, disembodied consciousness or spirit, for example) and this sense is useful for helping the mage to know when she is spied upon or just watched by a being currently lurking in refined dimensions.&quot; - MtAw 1st Ed. Core (206) - <i>\u200bSee also Sense Life, Detect Substance, Spirit Tongue, and Speak with the Dead and so on as well...</i></span></span><br /> <br /> \u200b</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\">The one line that mucks up my thinking is in paragraph 3 above (&quot;...visible or audible to the mage but not in his presence...&quot;). On the one hand, I understand the designers (and ST's) not wanting sensory magic to make all spells 200-yard range spells (such as performing a Psychic Assault on a person in their living room 150 yards away from the mage sitting on a park bench reading a newspaper) regardless of circumstance... but I also see an absolute interpretation of <i><b>\u200bnot in his presence </b></i>\u200bresulting in opinions like: &quot;The target made a good stealth roll, and is inside a cardboard box you can't see through, so you can't sense his mind,&quot; or &quot;You sense his mind just on the other side of this door, but since you can't physically see him with your actual eyes, you need to use space conjunctionally to affect him in any way,&quot; or &quot;'In your presence' means literally in the same room as you and not concealed by any intervening surface.&quot;<br /> <br /> \u200bAs a jest, I imagine a poor ST saying: &quot;Well... the target raised a blanket up between you. You'll need to use space conjunctionally to affect her in any way with magic because you can't physically see her directly with your mundane senses...&quot;<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#231f20\">So, with the logic of the various quotes above, am I right in believing that a mage using Magic to sense a target that is </span><i><b><span style=\"color:#231f20\">within</span></b></i><span style=\"color:#231f20\"> 200 yards (but behind a barrier such as a wall or in the space just below the mage) is then capable of affecting that target </span><b><i><span style=\"color:#231f20\">without</span></i></b><span style=\"color:#231f20\"> using sympathetic magic, but </span><b><i><span style=\"color:#231f20\">with</span></i></b><span style=\"color:#231f20\"> penalties to the rolls from concealment?<br /> <br /> \u200bDoes the thickness and make-up of the intervening materials make a difference? In other words, does a mundane but reinforced wall with lead sheeting provide more interference than the standard in-home drywall? When you have senses that pierce the veils of reality (as with Mind, Spirit, and Death), can a simple physical barrier act as an insurmountable shield? I also understand that some beings possess powers and innate abilities all on their own which are designed to confound such senses...<br /> <br /> \u200bTo be very specific, I don't want to be completely unimpeded because my character possesses sensory magic, but neither do I want to face arbitrary or inconsistent results.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13169,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13169-drkorpal","user":"DrKorpal","date":". 03-26-2017, 12:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13169-drkorpal","name":"DrKorpal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T19:45:54-07:00","id":1068517,"title":"","content":"I'm sensing that this might be a difficult question...<br /> <br /> \u200bThat leads me to believe that this is something many folks might have questions about themselves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13169-drkorpal","name":"DrKorpal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T21:51:55-07:00","id":1068548,"title":"","content":"My first thought is to say that if your senses alone can let you know where the target is they are sensory targets. So if you can hear them breathing through the door, go for it.<br /> Perhaps ask yourself if they could be hit with an aimed spell, dependent more upon the ability to 'aim' than obstacles in the way.<br /> \u200bIn the case of the man in a box, is the box big enough you can't guess where he is inside it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T22:20:24-07:00","id":1068552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrKorpal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1068319-mtaw-1st-edition-sensory-range-sensory-magic-and-the-200-yard-rule?p=1068517#post1068517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm sensing that this might be a difficult question...<br /> <br /> \u200bThat leads me to believe that this is something many folks might have questions about themselves.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it's more that 2e is out now and in that they ditch the 200 yard thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T00:27:56-07:00","id":1068571,"title":"","content":"I was constantly arguing with my players about this in 1st edition, the number of times they tried to use Life/Mind Knowing spells to detect somebody and then cast on them, and the number of times i had to explain that while they knew somebody was there they werent sensing them was annoying, then the arguments about using binoculars greater than 200 yards and all that..one of the things im glad isnt in 2nd edition actually.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T19:32:42-07:00","id":1068986,"title":"","content":"I like the idea of allowing a reasonable circumventing of close obstacles (like a wall or two) with increasing die penalties... IF the character can sense the target magically.<br /> <br /> That's no different than targeting someone (with penalties for concealment) on the other side of a wall because you can hear them yelling at someone else, even though you can't see them.<br /> <br /> It's not overly powerful to allow for it. Take 2 mages: Gnosis3, Mind 3, Space 2, and 3's in relevant stats/skills.<br /> Mage 1 tries to perform a Psychic Assault on mage 2 from the neighboring apartment. She starts with 9 dice, subtracting dice for concealment (say -2 dice per intervening wall). Let's say, 2 walls for -4 dice... We're now at 5 dice to roll, but it's important, so she used a tool, high speech, and spends willpower... Back up to 11 dice. Let's go with 4 successes on the roll. Then subtract the target's Mind Shield, bringing the successes to 1...<br /> One point of bashing that will heal in 15 minutes (or less; instant with Life), and the target is now fully aware of her presence. From a total possible dice pool of 15, down to a single point of bashing... not an effective choice. And other resistable effects will be even less effective.<br /> <br /> As to the 200-yard rule: 1) it isn't absolute; 2) it isn't improved mechanically [via optics].<br /> I read it as a cap on the range of magical affects of any type - unless modified by a conjunctional casting with Space 2+.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13169-drkorpal","name":"DrKorpal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T21:06:58-07:00","id":1069016,"title":"","content":"If I were still playing 1E, I would house rule in the 2E rules for range because they're simpler to apply. vOv<br /> <br /> Under the 2E rules, if the character can sense the target without utilize electronic or magical remote viewing, that's sensory range. Hearing them on the other side of a wall or two is still sensory range, even if it requires magically-enhanced hearing, and there's no penalty involved. Seeing them with binoculars, sensory range.<br /> <br /> It makes for better pacing in my opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","user":"galivet","date":". 03-28-2017, 12:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T21:55:16-07:00","id":1069067,"title":"","content":"I like that better, too. But I'm in a 1.0 group. Maybe I can convince the ST to switch out some mechanics...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13169-drkorpal","name":"DrKorpal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T23:19:28-07:00","id":1069147,"title":"","content":"2ed sensory range becomes especially important when you start dealing with Forces weather spells.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-28T08:01:57-07:00","id":1069359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrKorpal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1068319-mtaw-1st-edition-sensory-range-sensory-magic-and-the-200-yard-rule#post1068319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#231f20\">So, with the logic of the various quotes above, am I right in believing that a mage using Magic to sense a target that is </span><i><b><span style=\"color:#231f20\">within</span></b></i><span style=\"color:#231f20\"> 200 yards (but behind a barrier such as a wall or in the space just below the mage) is then capable of affecting that target </span><b><i><span style=\"color:#231f20\">without</span></i></b><span style=\"color:#231f20\"> using sympathetic magic, but </span><b><i><span style=\"color:#231f20\">with</span></i></b><span style=\"color:#231f20\"> penalties to the rolls from concealment?<br /> <br /> \u200bDoes the thickness and make-up of the intervening materials make a difference? In other words, does a mundane but reinforced wall with lead sheeting provide more interference than the standard in-home drywall? When you have senses that pierce the veils of reality (as with Mind, Spirit, and Death), can a simple physical barrier act as an insurmountable shield? I also understand that some beings possess powers and innate abilities all on their own which are designed to confound such senses...<br /> <br /> \u200bTo be very specific, I don't want to be completely unimpeded because my character possesses sensory magic, but neither do I want to face arbitrary or inconsistent results.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?</span></div> </div> </div> </div>The answer is tucked away in the definition of what type of magic is being used; specifically, Unveiling spells impart <b>sensory</b> information:<br /> <br /> <i><b>Gain sensory perception</b> of phenomena within the Arcanum\u2019s purview. Atlanteans called this the Practice of Unveiling.</i><br /> -- Mage: the Awakening, p.131<br /> <br /> <i>While <b>Unveiling imparts sensory knowledge</b>, Knowing allows a willworker to delve into the lore of the Mysteries.</i><br /> -- Tome of the Mysteries, p.19<br /> <br /> <i>If a mystic effect <b>imparts sensory perception</b> of the phenomenon in question to the mage, then it is an Unveiling effect.</i><br /> -- Tome of the Mysteries, p.20<br /> <br /> To wit; Sensory Range can be extended by magic out to the 200 yard limit with Unveiling spells.<br /> <br /> Whether or not penalties are assessed would probably depend on the specific circumstances and spell used, but this would be a function of the spell effect (Example: How many corners a Space 2 Angle Vision went around).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1068319-mtaw-1st-edition-sensory-range-sensory-magic-and-the-200-yard-rule?p=1068571#post1068571\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...the number of times they tried to use Life/Mind Knowing spells to detect somebody and then cast on them...</div> </div> </div> </div>This is addressed by RAW. Note that most of the spells that reveal in-area out-of-senses information <b>are</b> Knowing spells (such as Sense Life or Sense Consciousness) which <i>specifically</i> <i>do not</i> impart Sensory information:<br /> <br /> <i>An important detail to note is that <b>Knowing magics specifically</b> <b>do not bestow sensory information</b> upon the mage. The Practice of Knowing deals in facts, not the senses.</i><br /> -- Tome of the Mysteries, p.19<br /> <br /> Using the rule of thumb that Sensory Range can only be extended via Unveiling should address most situations. Hope that helps.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3557,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3557-grey_goose","user":"grey_goose","date":". 03-28-2017, 11:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3557-grey_goose","name":"grey_goose","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-28T10:43:08-07:00","id":1069458,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>grey_goose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1068319-mtaw-1st-edition-sensory-range-sensory-magic-and-the-200-yard-rule?p=1069359#post1069359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The answer is tucked away in the definition of what type of magic is being used; specifically, Unveiling spells impart <b>sensory</b> information:<br /> <br /> <i><b>Gain sensory perception</b> of phenomena within the Arcanum\u2019s purview. Atlanteans called this the Practice of Unveiling.</i><br /> -- Mage: the Awakening, p.131<br /> <br /> <i>While <b>Unveiling imparts sensory knowledge</b>, Knowing allows a willworker to delve into the lore of the Mysteries.</i><br /> -- Tome of the Mysteries, p.19<br /> <br /> <i>If a mystic effect <b>imparts sensory perception</b> of the phenomenon in question to the mage, then it is an Unveiling effect.</i><br /> -- Tome of the Mysteries, p.20<br /> <br /> To wit; Sensory Range can be extended by magic out to the 200 yard limit with Unveiling spells.<br /> <br /> Whether or not penalties are assessed would probably depend on the specific circumstances and spell used, but this would be a function of the spell effect (Example: How many corners a Space 2 Angle Vision went around).<br /> <br /> <br /> This is addressed by RAW. Note that most of the spells that reveal in-area out-of-senses information <b>are</b> Knowing spells (such as Sense Life or Sense Consciousness) which <i>specifically</i> <i>do not</i> impart Sensory information:<br /> <br /> <b><i>An important detail to note is that Knowing magics specifically <b>do not bestow sensory information</b> upon the mage. The Practice of Knowing deals in facts, not the senses.</i><br /> -- Tome of the Mysteries, p.19</b><br /> <br /> <b>Using the rule of thumb that Sensory Range can only be extended via Unveiling should address most situations. Hope that helps.</b></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> This is perfectly helpful. Thank you! Fortunately, our ST is a very reasonable guy. He's happy to change opinions or decisions IF the changes are supported by the rules (and even occasionally if not, if it moves the story along or adds the right kind of flavor without harming the systems in place). And I dislike having an incomplete understanding of rules important to my style of play.<br /> <br /> I'll share this info with my ST... he'll appreciate it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13169-drkorpal","name":"DrKorpal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-28T11:50:00-07:00","id":1069516,"title":"","content":"Awesome, glad to help and happy you found it useful! Mage's biggest problem is that the rules are reaaaally spread out and take a lot of digging and cross-referencing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3557-grey_goose","name":"grey_goose","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-29T14:28:45-07:00","id":1070299,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>grey_goose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1068319-mtaw-1st-edition-sensory-range-sensory-magic-and-the-200-yard-rule?p=1069516#post1069516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Awesome, glad to help and happy you found it useful! Mage's biggest problem is that the rules are reaaaally spread out and take a lot of digging and cross-referencing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. They really should provide an online compilation of rules: all data sets (Patha, Orders, Rotes [by Arcanum], merits, etc.)... but most importantly, the Systems rules.<br /> <br /> At the very least, there should be a list showing where relevant data can be found in the books (&quot;Rules on [X] can be found in [title/page; title/page; title/page]).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13169-drkorpal","name":"DrKorpal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}